<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Himachal Pradesh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Himachal Pradesh is a mountainous state of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a>. It was created in 1948 as a union of some thirty princely hill states, including Chamba, Mandi and Sirmur, absorbed the hill districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1966 and became a full state in 1971. It is known for its orchards, hydroelectric power and hill resorts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh.jpg" alt="Himachal Pradesh" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UnpetitproleX · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kullu_Valley_near_Manali,_Himachal_Pradesh,_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Himachal Pradesh lies in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> and is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s northernmost state. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and Ladakh to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a> to the south-west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a> to the south-east, and it shares an international border with the Tibet Autonomous Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to the east. It covers 55,673 square kilometres. The Zanskar range runs in the north-east, the Dhauladhar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pir-panjal-range">Pir Panjal</a> ranges form much of the core, and the Shiwalik range forms the south and west. Reo Purgyil, at 6,816 metres, is the highest peak. The name means snowy slopes, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> hima, snow, and acala, slopes or abode.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tribes such as the Koli, Hali, Dagi, Dhaugri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dasa">Dasa</a>, Khasa, Kanaura and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirata">Kirata</a> inhabited the region from prehistoric times, and the foothills were inhabited by people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>. For most of its history the region was ruled by local kingdoms, some of which accepted the suzerainty of larger empires. Before independence it comprised the hilly regions of the Punjab Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>.</p><p>After independence the Chief Commissioner's Province of Himachal Pradesh was organised on 15 April 1948 by the integration of 30 small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> with four southern hill states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. The State of Bilaspur was merged into it on 1 July 1954. It later became a union territory, hilly areas of neighbouring Punjab were merged into it in 1966, and it was granted full statehood in 1971.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> is the summer capital and Dharamshala the winter capital. The state is divided into 12 districts. Agriculture, horticulture, hydropower and tourism are the main parts of its economy, and its perennial rivers supply hydroelectric plants. Hill stations such as Shimla, Manali, Dharamshala and Dalhousie attract visitors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Himachal Pradesh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bon’s leading monastery in India is the refounded Menri at Dolanji in <b>Himachal Pradesh</b>, and in Nepal Triten Norbutse stands on the western outskirts of Kathmandu.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bon">Bon · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>India’s constitution lists Sanskrit among the languages of the nation, and the states of Uttarakhand and <b>Himachal Pradesh</b> have made it a second official language.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Baghal joined the new province of <b>Himachal Pradesh</b> in 1948.</blockquote><a href="/state/baghal-state">Baghal State</a></li><li><blockquote>Baghat merged into <b>Himachal Pradesh</b> in April 1948, and Solan is now the headquarters of Solan district.</blockquote><a href="/state/baghat-state">Baghat State</a></li><li><blockquote>Balsan merged into <b>Himachal Pradesh</b> in April 1948 and is now part of Shimla district.</blockquote><a href="/state/balsan-state">Balsan State</a></li><li><blockquote>Bashahr merged into <b>Himachal Pradesh</b> in April 1948.</blockquote><a href="/state/bashahr-state">Bashahr State</a></li></ul></section>
